00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
The topic of our conversation today,

42
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
so you, Henry and I, a few weeks ago,

43
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,500
published a report that we've been working on for some time.

44
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:05,500
I think it was a question that we'd all grappled with over the last year plus,

45
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,500
where we were trying to get a sense of

46
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:12,500
what matters when you're valuing DeFi protocols, DeFi apps.

47
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:16,500
People talk about TVL a lot, total value locked,

48
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:20,000
which I think you've seen businesses like Turtle Club and Royco,

49
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:28,000
which are explicitly capital raising tools for DeFi protocols and apps.

50
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,000
You've seen the success of them.

51
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,500
I think a lot of people question how important that actually is

52
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:40,000
and whether it's worthwhile and actually additive to the core value of these things.

53
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
It's funny because in a few ways,

54
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,500
it does remind me of the conversations people were having in the early internet era

55
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:52,000
when they were saying, is users the important metric of a marketplace

56
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
or of a social application or is it total process volume

57
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
or is it number of website visits or is it number of different offerings or SKUs?

58
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
So I don't think it's uncommon to have this conversation about

59
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
what actually matters in these emerging business types.

60
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,000
But it comes at a funny time because we put this out in late October.

61
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The lending markets were the topic of focus

62
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
and since then, the total value locked in these lending markets has shuttered.

63
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,500
You look at the chart and it's a pretty direct downward inflection.

64
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,000
Altcoin markets have been severely challenged, especially since then.

65
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,500
So I have my views which we'll dive into,

66
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,500
but where do you think that leaves us overall?

67
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:34,500
It's a good point.

68
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:38,500
The lending markets to me are specifically interesting in crypto

69
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:42,000
because they are one of the most sustainable businesses.

70
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,000
And I think what you're pointing out is that there is still a lot of noise there.

71
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Why are they sustainable in your view?

72
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
We can zoom out a little bit and talk about the business models in crypto in general.

73
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,500
Broadly speaking, most crypto falls into DeFi.

74
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:00,000
And the DeFi stack splits into what I would consider two major categories.

75
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
One has to do with crypto's use for tokenization.

76
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
So that's settlement, that's swapping.

77
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:09,500
Those are these activities that are instantaneous, one-time use activities.

78
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:11,500
And then the other is capital formation.

79
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,500
So this has really been the year of capital formation.

80
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:18,500
You see this with the rapid iteration of launchpads that we've seen,

81
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:20,500
but you also see this in lending.

82
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:25,500
And the tokenization side, the swapping and the settlement, very cyclical business.

83
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:29,500
This business is almost like there's a summer aspect to it, DeFi summer.

84
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,000
That's why we call it that.

85
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,500
With capital formation, this is like an evergreen business.

86
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:38,000
This is one thing that crypto directly serves the value prop of,

87
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
and you really can't do this capital formation outside of crypto.

88
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
And what are you describing in this context as capital formation?

89
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,000
There's two forms.

90
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
I mean, today we're going to talk about the lending form.

91
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
The other form is raising capital.

92
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
And I think that's what's going on with MetaDao,

93
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
what went on with PumpFun, all of that was super interesting.

94
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And it's a great business, but the lending markets have this really, really sticky user base.

95
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:05,500
And this user base is fundamentally just seeking yield on their assets.

96
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:09,500
They're trying to turn non-productive assets into productive assets.

97
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,500
And this is similar to stablecoin volume.

98
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:13,500
This doesn't go away with the season.

99
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,500
This is constantly something people are seeking to do.

100
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:21,500
So that's one of the reasons I think lending is one of the more durable businesses in crypto.

101
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:25,500
And we can dive all into the different types of lending, but that's what makes it interesting.

102
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:30,500
With the TVL drop off, I think this cuts into a recent trend that we've seen,

103
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:35,500
which is the forms of lending and borrowing to use cases that we see on chain

104
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:39,500
have completely changed over the past year to two years.

105
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,500
So originally, if you look at what lending started as, it started as CDPs,

106
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:47,500
where you would put up money and you would get a synthetic dollar essentially against this.

107
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:49,500
I'm going to roll you back for a second.

108
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:55,500
I actually don't think you can talk even about how well the defile lending platforms

109
00:05:55,500 --> 00:06:02,500
like Aave and Morpho have done without talking about the void that was left by Celsius and BlockFi

110
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:07,500
that had built what's probably the most straightforward use case of crypto,

111
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:14,500
which is I want to own Bitcoin and as much more Bitcoin as I can and less dollars,

112
00:06:14,500 --> 00:06:17,500
but have spendable dollars against that Bitcoin.

113
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,500
I think it's that cut and simple.

114
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:24,500
And I think a big part of the success of those platforms was the fact that

115
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,500
Celsius and BlockFi and others not only went away,

116
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:35,500
but bred mistrust in those platforms and the defi platforms benefited from that.

117
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,500
Yeah, and I think we can talk a lot about trust.

118
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:42,500
There's still a degree of trust me that goes into the platforms today.

119
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:47,500
And that's one of the things that we went into in this piece is rapidly these on chain platforms,

120
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:50,500
they're auditable, you can see what's going on there,

121
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:55,500
but you almost need a PhD today to actually understand the risk that you're getting paid for.

122
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:58,500
And this is fundamental to the lending markets is it's not risk free.

123
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,500
It's as close to the risk free rate as you can get.

124
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:06,500
So you go back to Celsius and you have this huge need to unlock capital

125
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:11,500
that just sitting there in these long positions that people want to use,

126
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:14,500
they want to unlock and for whatever reason,

127
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:19,500
whether it's taxes or whether it's just fundamentally that they want to hold on to it ideologically,

128
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:22,500
they go to a lender to try to unlock the capital.

129
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:24,500
This use case is still pretty persistent.

130
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:29,500
And this is probably one of the most durable trades that we see on lending markets today.

131
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:31,500
I mean, it has to be.

132
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:33,500
It's extremely durable.

133
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:36,500
So like a great example of this is the Morpho integration with Coinbase.

134
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:41,500
So Morpho is integrated with Coinbase so you can borrow against your CBBTC,

135
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:43,500
which is Coinbase's wrapped Bitcoin.

136
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:48,500
And this has just been growing exponentially since they started earlier this year.

137
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,500
It's over one and a half billion dollars of collateral in Bitcoin.

138
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:56,500
And this use case, when you look at the TVL chart in the drop off after October,

139
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,500
this has not been hindered at all.

140
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:04,500
What you do see in the drop off after October is another strategy that we saw come out recently,

141
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,500
which is this idea of looping.

142
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:10,500
For those who haven't listened to Henry and Corrin's podcast about the vaults,

143
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,500
this is extremely relevant for this.

144
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:16,500
But the general idea of looping is I have some yield bearing token.

145
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:19,500
The yield might be coming from any of many sources.

146
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:26,500
Treasury yield, right staking yield, basis trade through USDE, RWA lending, any of these.

147
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:33,500
And the idea or the realization that lending markets had is they can lend to these at a pretty high LTV.

148
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:37,500
And when you lend to them at a really high LTV, you can actually recycle that capital.

149
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:42,500
So you put in asset, you borrow against it, stablecoin, you buy asset,

150
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:47,500
you put asset back in and you borrow against it and you continue to do this.

151
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:52,500
And what you can do is you can take a yield from maybe 10 percent on an unlevered basis

152
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:55,500
and lever it up to call it 25, 30 percent.

153
00:08:55,500 --> 00:09:01,500
So this is what we really saw leading into October when we released this piece.

154
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,500
And admittedly, this was very scary.

155
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:07,500
And the reason this was very scary was because it's really two reasons.

156
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:15,500
One is although crypto is extremely traceable, it's virtually impossible to identify how levered a system is.

157
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:21,500
It's very possible to go and do this looping on multiple platforms or from multiple accounts.

158
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:24,500
So this is not something that's easily traceable, not just to us,

159
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:29,500
but we spoke to all of the major lending protocols and all of them agreed that they don't know themselves.

160
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:31,500
It's not something that they can measure.

161
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:38,500
The second thing that's scary about this is the underlying increasingly went down the risk curve.

162
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:43,500
And what I mean by this is USDE is a relatively vanilla strategy.

163
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:47,500
Ever since I've been at L1D, we've been getting pitched basis trade funds.

164
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:50,500
And this was super popular in 2021.

165
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:54,500
When USDE came out, it just evaporated the basis trade fund pitch.

166
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,500
We stopped seeing them entirely.

167
00:09:56,500 --> 00:10:02,500
And the reason being is because USDE essentially commodified that trade and they wrapped it in a token

168
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:05,500
and they made it so anybody can really access it with way less friction.

169
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:07,500
And they handle the infrastructure.

170
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:11,500
Now, this is something that a lot of crypto people are familiar with the risks on.

171
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:13,500
So they're comfortable underwriting it.

172
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:20,500
So when you see USDE being 20% of the Aave lending pool, you don't want the concentration risk, perhaps,

173
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,500
but you're okay with the underlying risk.

174
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:28,500
What we saw after this is this implementation of this strategy across different vault strategies.

175
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:32,500
And by vault strategies, I really mean this idea of putting your tokens into a vault

176
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:36,500
and them running some sort of trading strategy beneath it.

177
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:39,500
There are levels of obfuscation that people use.

178
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:45,500
So going into the Elixir event, what we discovered is essentially these vaults were just cross-depositing into themselves.

179
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:52,500
And this is extremely dangerous because essentially you take one fund

180
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:55,500
and you're spreading the same amount of risk across all the other funds.

181
00:10:55,500 --> 00:11:01,500
So this was a strategy that I think we really rightfully identified as not extremely durable.

182
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:04,500
It was very profitable for a lot of lending protocols.

183
00:11:04,500 --> 00:11:07,500
It will continue to be a strategy that lending protocols enable.

184
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:13,500
But this is not what I would consider to be the bet on lending markets, so to speak.

185
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:17,500
I fully agree. And you left me with a few different points.

186
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:21,500
I first off want to replay why we're having this conversation

187
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:25,500
and then some of the key points you brought up, which were you and I,

188
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:31,500
as long-term investors in the business of finding what the good and lasting business models are,

189
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:35,500
and we're looking at lending in crypto as one case of those,

190
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:38,500
because of what we think is a market gap that'll grow,

191
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:42,500
and because of what we think could be strengths of why that business model is durable,

192
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:46,500
which you made points on, and then in examining those lending protocols,

193
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:50,500
trying to pick out what's important and vetting the risks to your point,

194
00:11:50,500 --> 00:11:54,500
and that you see these metrics like total value locked inflecting up,

195
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:56,500
but you don't have a sense of how much leverage is in the system.

196
00:11:56,500 --> 00:12:02,500
And what I've thought is a very funny characteristic of the current crypto market

197
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:07,500
is for so long, composability or all the apps working well together

198
00:12:07,500 --> 00:12:10,500
was such a north star that people wanted.

199
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:14,500
And lo and behold, as a result of that, you get very composable risk,

200
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:18,500
which means that one of these yield-bearing stablecoins becomes insolvent,

201
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:20,500
and it's a big problem for the lending protocol.

202
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:23,500
It's a big problem for whoever's curating that lending pool.

203
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:27,500
It's a big problem for assets that are in a vault with those assets.

204
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:29,500
It's a problem for everything else around.

205
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:33,500
I want to touch on those points specifically, but I did want to replay.

206
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:38,500
That's our core exploration here, and I wanted to ask you on the record,

207
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:44,500
do you think that 1010 happened because of this leverage profile that we're talking about,

208
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:48,500
1010 being the major crypto washout that happened a few weeks back?

209
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,500
That's a really good question.

210
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:52,500
I hope you share your thoughts after.

211
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:57,500
So if you look at 1010, I think that this risk played a major role

212
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:03,500
in how conservative the perpetual exchanges were with their own risk.

213
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:07,500
And what I mean by that specifically is leading into 1010,

214
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:13,500
you had call it $6 to $8 billion of USD in Aave.

215
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:19,500
And Aave is by any definition probably the most systemically important protocol in crypto.

216
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:27,500
And this amount of USD being essentially pushed into the big risk house in crypto

217
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:33,500
made it so that any tremors in this asset would have huge, huge, huge downstream effects.

218
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:37,500
So Aave went as far as to peg USD to USDT.

219
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:39,500
This is an extreme measure.

220
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:42,500
I wouldn't recommend hard pegging any asset in the lending market.

221
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,500
But this turned out to be extremely necessary in this case,

222
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:52,500
because what ended up, as far as we can tell, causing 1010 was a poor liquidation of USDE on Binance,

223
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:59,500
an order in the amount of maybe $60 million that was dumped on an order book that was not $60 million deep.

224
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:04,500
And Binance's Oracle being tied into USDE order book on its own platform.

225
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:12,500
So the question as to what caused 1010, I think the direct answer is probably this bad liquidation.

226
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:17,500
But the indirect answer, it could definitely be ascribed to the fear that was around USDE,

227
00:14:17,500 --> 00:14:20,500
the fear that was around this heavily levered asset.

228
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:21,500
What do you think?

229
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:23,500
I think that a lot of that is true.

230
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:27,500
I think the liquidation piece spurred it in the moment, if that makes sense.

231
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:32,500
I'm going to be pretty harsh on a lot of the assets in the industry at this moment,

232
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:40,500
which is to say that I think the degree of pain felt was because of how thin a lot of the order book depth is

233
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,500
on a lot of these assets and across a lot of venues,

234
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:50,500
with the outcome being that you have an asset that let's say it trades at a $200 million market cap

235
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:54,500
and supposedly it has $5 million a daily volume.

236
00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:57,500
But the order book depth within reason is a couple percent.

237
00:14:57,500 --> 00:15:03,500
So realistically, you can only trade a couple hundred thousand something like that of an asset like this

238
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:06,500
without materially moving the market, probably less actually.

239
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:07,500
But let's call it that.

240
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:12,500
The outcome of that is that if you do have an initial liquidation of size

241
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:18,500
or you have a market tremor that distresses a sizable market actor,

242
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:24,500
and they need to start liquidating or selling assets across a set of assets in the ecosystem,

243
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:30,500
because those order books are not very deep, the price of these assets are going to fall really fast

244
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:38,500
to the point where someone could easily argue a lot of these assets probably aren't worth what their claimed market cap is

245
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:43,500
in the sense that yes, the price of the asset as it trades on exchanges

246
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:48,500
times the number of tokens in circulation is that $200 million market cap,

247
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:52,500
but you can only functionally trade $250,000 of the asset per day.

248
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:55,500
And if not, the market moves 10, 20 percent.

249
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:58,500
I think we have a lot of assets like that in our ecosystem.

250
00:15:58,500 --> 00:16:03,500
This is fundamentally the fear and the cloud that's over everybody's head right now

251
00:16:03,500 --> 00:16:11,500
is when you look at all of the charts today, you have to zoom out because we now have this 95 percent candle on Tencent.

252
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:16,500
You're reminded that the fundamental floor is not necessarily there for a lot of assets.

253
00:16:16,500 --> 00:16:24,500
But the thing that I think you want to hone in on and the real question for people in crypto is, are any of these assets worth anything?

254
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:30,500
Definitely. And one question I was going to ask you from your seat, because you guys invest in funds as well.

255
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:37,500
How do you think about investing in funds that are going to invest in these long tail of crypto tokens

256
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:41,500
that are going to mark them or report what they're worth to you guys?

257
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:47,500
And let's say they own $10 million of a token that trades at $200 million, the fictional token that I mentioned.

258
00:16:47,500 --> 00:16:49,500
They can't recognize that value.

259
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:54,500
So how do you think about having this portfolio of managers and tokens of value that, to your point,

260
00:16:54,500 --> 00:16:57,500
is difficult to liquidate, doesn't have a fundamental floor?

261
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:58,500
How do you navigate that?

262
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:00,500
This is a question we deal with every day.

263
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:06,500
And honestly, this is a really strong case for why a fund of funds makes sense in crypto

264
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:12,500
is because we see many funds that will mark a position that we see to be a position,

265
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:18,500
maybe its FTV is $500 million and it has $25,000 of volume per day.

266
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:21,500
And they'll mark this at the value of $500 million.

267
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:26,500
With our managers, we have very strict, essentially, valuation policies built in.

268
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:32,500
So for extremely illiquid assets, we're talking about assets that just came into existence

269
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:35,500
that might have a 10% float or something like this.

270
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:38,500
You have to put a massive discount on these assets.

271
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:43,500
And the reason we have to do that is not because we think that it's necessarily the right thing to do.

272
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:48,500
It's because we're economically incentivized as allocators to make sure that we're not overpaying in fees.

273
00:17:48,500 --> 00:17:53,500
If you take a malicious example here, a fund could easily inflate their NAV

274
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:56,500
and charge you the fees on that performance.

275
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:57,500
Does that happen?

276
00:17:57,500 --> 00:17:58,500
Of course it happens.

277
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:00,500
How do those conversations go?

278
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:03,500
I think this is something people in the industry probably don't know a ton about.

279
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:05,500
So generally, there's two types of funds.

280
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:08,500
Ray from L&D speaks about this all the time.

281
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:10,500
There's the GBB and GPA.

282
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:15,500
You can, in not so many words, categorize them as whether they want management fees or performance fees.

283
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:18,500
The number one thing you can do to avoid having to deal with this issue in the first place

284
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:21,500
is go for people who are looking for performance fees.

285
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:26,500
Because any good fund out there knows that if they overinflate their book on an asset

286
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:29,500
that's not really worth that much and it draws down,

287
00:18:29,500 --> 00:18:32,500
that their high watermark is too high for them to get over.

288
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:35,500
And explain why that's a problem because it's an important point.

289
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:38,500
A problem for us or a problem for them?

290
00:18:38,500 --> 00:18:39,500
Problem for them.

291
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:41,500
Felipe talked about this when he was on.

292
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:45,500
The general concept of the high watermark I think isn't really well known to a lot of non-LPs,

293
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:50,500
but essentially if you bring your NAV up to 100 and then it drops back down

294
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:52,500
and then it goes up partially to 100,

295
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:57,500
you're not making performance fees on this until you breach what your high watermark was,

296
00:18:57,500 --> 00:19:01,500
call it that 100 mark, unless you bring in net new capital or whatever.

297
00:19:01,500 --> 00:19:06,500
So in this circumstance, if you're a manager and you want to incentivize really good talent,

298
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:09,500
you're not going to be able to pay that talent.

299
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:17,500
So it is trivially easy in crypto to go into a token, have it launch, have it go up 10x,

300
00:19:17,500 --> 00:19:19,500
but be completely locked for like a year.

301
00:19:19,500 --> 00:19:23,500
And then you go to your fund admin and you say it's worth whatever that 10x value is.

302
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:29,500
And all of a sudden your investors have to pay fees on that unrealized, essentially on what that is.

303
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:31,500
As a liquid fund, to be clear.

304
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:32,500
Yeah, as a liquid fund.

305
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:34,500
As a venture fund, that's not an issue.

306
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:35,500
You get paid on the way out.

307
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:38,500
As a liquid fund, the fees are crystallized annually, essentially.

308
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:42,500
If you draw down below that high watermark, all of your talent is going to leave

309
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:45,500
because they're going to recognize that they're not going to have the big payday that they wanted.

310
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:48,500
So it's in your favor to market conservatively.

311
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:52,500
And once that asset gets to a mature level where you can actually liquidate it safely,

312
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:53,500
that's when you get paid.

313
00:19:53,500 --> 00:19:56,500
So this is what smart managers, this is what the good managers do.

314
00:19:57,500 --> 00:20:01,500
Malicious managers or negligent managers will allow this to happen to their fund.

315
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:03,500
And one of two things will happen.

316
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:05,500
Either their team leaves or their investors leave.

317
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:10,500
With that in mind that you have these assets that trade at these multiples,

318
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:15,500
do you think it's just a reality that as we get better market discovery,

319
00:20:15,500 --> 00:20:18,500
a lot of these assets will trade down to lower prices

320
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:23,500
just because they're finding an actual value once you take away an illiquidity premium?

321
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:24,500
No, I don't think so.

322
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:28,500
And the reason I don't think so is because there are many ways to construct a value.

323
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:34,500
When you look at TradFi, it's not like you see extremely rational valuations either.

324
00:20:34,500 --> 00:20:36,500
So why do things trade where they do?

325
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:37,500
There are many reasons.

326
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:42,500
Depending on what the asset is, there would be different things that go into the valuation.

327
00:20:42,500 --> 00:20:44,500
To tie it back to the lending markets, for example,

328
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:48,500
one of the reasons we did this piece in the first place is because you see that

329
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:53,500
a lot of these lending protocols are valued seemingly on a ratio to their TVL.

330
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:57,500
And this does not mean that the market's irrational.

331
00:20:57,500 --> 00:21:01,500
This just means that the market is probably valuing it on the wrong thing.

332
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:03,500
It actually indicates that the market is rational.

333
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:04,500
Collectively.

334
00:21:04,500 --> 00:21:06,500
Yeah, there are groupings.

335
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:11,500
So you have Aave trading relative to Morpho based on TVL.

336
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:15,500
But when you dig into what the business of Aave or Morpho really is,

337
00:21:15,500 --> 00:21:17,500
it's a business of loans.

338
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:20,500
It's not a business of how many dollars you can put in a smart contract.

339
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:23,500
It's a business of how many dollars you take out of a smart contract.

340
00:21:23,500 --> 00:21:26,500
And this is where the market needs to step up.

341
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:28,500
And I think where the market can really mature.

342
00:21:28,500 --> 00:21:34,500
Theo would tell you all about how they approach valuation for protocols like this.

343
00:21:34,500 --> 00:21:38,500
But fundamentally, it comes down to figuring out what is the revenue driving activity,

344
00:21:38,500 --> 00:21:42,500
what is a meaningful activity in the protocol, and valuing it based on that.

345
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:47,500
Another really good example of this is if you go back to 2021, 2022, 2023,

346
00:21:47,500 --> 00:21:49,500
nobody really understood L1s.

347
00:21:49,500 --> 00:21:50,500
They still don't.

348
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:53,500
So the way that they would compare L1s was on two things.

349
00:21:53,500 --> 00:21:57,500
And I'm not innocent of this, but you would look at user metrics

350
00:21:57,500 --> 00:21:59,500
and you would look at GitHub commits.

351
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:04,500
And anybody today, if you told that to, will rightfully tell you that,

352
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:07,500
okay, user metrics is probably the worst metric in the world,

353
00:22:07,500 --> 00:22:10,500
because as soon as you start looking at it, everybody can fake it.

354
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:12,500
And then GitHub commits is not a great metric

355
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:16,500
because different companies are at different stages of developing that.

356
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:18,500
So there's a cyclicality to GitHub commits,

357
00:22:18,500 --> 00:22:21,500
where if you're going to be pushing Fire Dancer this month,

358
00:22:21,500 --> 00:22:23,500
you're going to have a ton of commits.

359
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:27,500
And there are ways to just hammer GitHub commits with no value?

360
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:32,500
There are programs out there that will allow you to code how you want your GitHub commits to look,

361
00:22:32,500 --> 00:22:37,500
so you can write things on your GitHub in the nice green squares.

362
00:22:37,500 --> 00:22:39,500
So yeah, these are extremely gameable metrics.

363
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:42,500
But what's not a gameable metric is revenue paying metrics.

364
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:47,500
At the very end of the day, somebody is paying that revenue.

365
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:51,500
This reminds me a bit, and I remember when we talked about this when we were doing this study,

366
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:57,500
of how in traditional finance you'll just have stock prices swing during periods

367
00:22:57,500 --> 00:23:03,500
where there's discovery of what bucket even a mature company should trade in

368
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:05,500
from a multiple perspective, where people will be like,

369
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:08,500
oh, it's not a SaaS business, it's a consulting business.

370
00:23:08,500 --> 00:23:11,500
So therefore, it shouldn't trade at 15 actions, it should trade at five,

371
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:13,500
and it'll bump down 60% a year or something.

372
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:17,500
Those things really happen, and we're seeing that kind of discovery in our industry.

373
00:23:17,500 --> 00:23:21,500
A great example of this is there's a bank out there who put a report out on Figure.

374
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:24,500
Figure went public, they're starting to get coverage by real banks,

375
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:27,500
and you scroll down to the comps, and in the comps section,

376
00:23:27,500 --> 00:23:32,500
you have a list of companies, including Coinbase and Circle.

377
00:23:32,500 --> 00:23:36,500
And the only thing that Coinbase, Circle, and Figure have in common

378
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:42,500
is Figure has a early stage exchange, which is not a big component to its revenue,

379
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:43,500
and they're in crypto.

380
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:46,500
But it's very much so in that phase that you're describing,

381
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:51,500
where you're trying to say, okay, so what P-E ratio should we assign to this?

382
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:53,500
What sector is this in?

383
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:59,500
At this point in crypto, we have a really good viewpoint on what the sectors are in crypto.

384
00:23:59,500 --> 00:24:02,500
I've tried the sector mapping exercise almost every year,

385
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:07,500
and it wasn't until the past two years where it became consensus with what everybody sees.

386
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:09,500
Which is what? Lending, trading?

387
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:12,500
So on the DeFi stack, you start with issuance.

388
00:24:12,500 --> 00:24:14,500
So issuance can take two forms.

389
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:18,500
Either you're issuing an asset that exists, like an RWA issuance platform,

390
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:21,500
Circle is a great example, or you're issuing a new asset,

391
00:24:21,500 --> 00:24:25,500
which would be a launchpad, MetaDao, Pump, Echo, all of these.

392
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:27,500
After you do issuance, you have settlement.

393
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:30,500
Settlement, very much so, is L1s.

394
00:24:30,500 --> 00:24:33,500
So you settle your assets on a blockchain.

395
00:24:33,500 --> 00:24:35,500
After settlement is swapping.

396
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:39,500
If you don't have swapping, you can't do anything else interesting at all in crypto.

397
00:24:39,500 --> 00:24:41,500
So swapping is one asset for another.

398
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:43,500
Everybody knows the swapping protocols.

399
00:24:43,500 --> 00:24:47,500
Once you have swapping, you can liquidate assets, and so you allow for lending.

400
00:24:47,500 --> 00:24:52,500
And then after lending becomes a new category that I like to call strategies.

401
00:24:52,500 --> 00:24:54,500
So this is this vault category.

402
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:56,500
This is like LIDO as well.

403
00:24:57,500 --> 00:25:01,500
I want to take an asset that is a non-productive asset, turn it into a productive asset.

404
00:25:01,500 --> 00:25:04,500
And then lastly, and I would put this adjacent to all of these,

405
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:09,500
is this category that I would call probably asset management or custody.

406
00:25:09,500 --> 00:25:12,500
And this is where we see you might have squads.

407
00:25:12,500 --> 00:25:15,500
You might also have copper and fire blocks.

408
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:18,500
You have a lot of C-Fi, D-Fi overlaps here.

409
00:25:18,500 --> 00:25:22,500
So broadly speaking, this is every single thing that you're going to do in crypto.

410
00:25:22,500 --> 00:25:24,500
It fits within these categories.

411
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:26,500
And you can have different flavors of this.

412
00:25:26,500 --> 00:25:27,500
You can have gaming.

413
00:25:27,500 --> 00:25:30,500
But in gaming, you will introduce these D-Fi elements,

414
00:25:30,500 --> 00:25:33,500
whether it's settlement, whether it's swaps, whether it's lending.

415
00:25:33,500 --> 00:25:35,500
In NFTs, you do the same thing.

416
00:25:35,500 --> 00:25:40,500
In D-PIN, you have, I'll be honest, primarily just issuance.

417
00:25:40,500 --> 00:25:44,500
But it's very likely that we'll get a step beyond this, like with USDAI,

418
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:48,500
where you have issuance going into lending, going into strategies, going into those things.

419
00:25:48,500 --> 00:25:51,500
So I think that sector map lays out pretty clearly there.

420
00:25:51,500 --> 00:25:53,500
Now that we have this sector map,

421
00:25:53,500 --> 00:25:57,500
it's the crypto community's job to start to underwrite these things more intelligently.

422
00:25:57,500 --> 00:26:01,500
So this is where you ask for more interesting metrics.

423
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:04,500
So one of the things that we pointed out in this piece is

424
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:09,500
you couldn't write this piece without pointing out what you think is the better metric for lending.

425
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:15,500
And I think it was pretty obvious to us that the most important metric in lending

426
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:18,500
is really the availability of stablecoins on that platform,

427
00:26:18,500 --> 00:26:21,500
whether it's USDC, whether it's USDT.

428
00:26:21,500 --> 00:26:25,500
These stablecoins are overwhelmingly what is being borrowed.

429
00:26:25,500 --> 00:26:30,500
So in order for a loan to exist, you need an asset that they're going to borrow against.

430
00:26:30,500 --> 00:26:36,500
And very few people want to borrow WBTC because they're effectively going short Bitcoin.

431
00:26:36,500 --> 00:26:39,500
Everybody in the world wants to lend WBTC.

432
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:45,500
You see this, there's $10 billion of WBTC sitting on Aave with nobody borrowing it.

433
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:49,500
And the reason being is because that eight basis points of borrow yield

434
00:26:49,500 --> 00:26:51,500
is the best that they're going to get out there.

435
00:26:51,500 --> 00:26:54,500
What I want to see in the lending protocols going forward

436
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:59,500
is essentially a valuation that is more hinged on how good are these teams at finding dollars?

437
00:26:59,500 --> 00:27:01,500
So it's capital raising.

438
00:27:01,500 --> 00:27:06,500
Exactly. Getting their dollars on platform so that they can be utilized.

439
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:08,500
And you mentioned revenue.

440
00:27:08,500 --> 00:27:10,500
I have my thoughts here.

441
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:16,500
I think there's very few, if any, crypto platforms that generate revenue

442
00:27:16,500 --> 00:27:19,500
that justifies most of the token valuations out there.

443
00:27:19,500 --> 00:27:22,500
And I'm curious if you feel otherwise,

444
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:26,500
or if you think these things even trade with any correlation to revenue.

445
00:27:26,500 --> 00:27:29,500
I'll answer that question with a question for you.

446
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:31,500
Out of the startups that you guys invest in,

447
00:27:31,500 --> 00:27:37,500
what percent of the startups you're investing in are profitable at the time of investment?

448
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:40,500
At the time of investment, very few. A minority.

449
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:42,500
A vast minority.

450
00:27:42,500 --> 00:27:48,500
I don't think investors are looking at these platforms to necessarily be profitable.

451
00:27:48,500 --> 00:27:52,500
Revenue generating is an indication that people want to use your platform.

452
00:27:52,500 --> 00:27:54,500
It's not a bulletproof indication.

453
00:27:54,500 --> 00:27:56,500
It is possible to fake revenue numbers.

454
00:27:56,500 --> 00:27:59,500
For instance, if you raise $2 billion,

455
00:27:59,500 --> 00:28:03,500
and then you essentially bleed that $2 billion in the form of using your own platform,

456
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:06,500
you could create $2 billion of revenue and then it will drop off the cliff.

457
00:28:06,500 --> 00:28:08,500
Or you'll succeed.

458
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:13,500
The reason people in crypto have been asking for revenue is really two things.

459
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:16,500
One is they've been asking for revenue in the form of buybacks

460
00:28:16,500 --> 00:28:19,500
because they're desperate for net new buyers of this token,

461
00:28:19,500 --> 00:28:21,500
and it seems like nobody's buying them.

462
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:26,500
And then the other reason is they don't trust the teams,

463
00:28:26,500 --> 00:28:28,500
and they want to see the real numbers.

464
00:28:28,500 --> 00:28:32,500
So if you're a team and you go through all of this venture raise,

465
00:28:32,500 --> 00:28:34,500
and you raise $50 million,

466
00:28:34,500 --> 00:28:37,500
and you aren't generating any revenue,

467
00:28:37,500 --> 00:28:40,500
then I think it's fair to say that you probably don't have PMF.

468
00:28:40,500 --> 00:28:44,500
But let's say you're a team that goes through the metadata launchpad,

469
00:28:44,500 --> 00:28:48,500
and it's day one after the launchpad you just got your first funding in.

470
00:28:48,500 --> 00:28:53,500
I think it's unreasonable to expect this team to be turning on big revenue numbers after day one.

471
00:28:53,500 --> 00:28:57,500
But a lot of these teams are carrying valuations well above $100 million,

472
00:28:57,500 --> 00:28:59,500
well above $250 million.

473
00:28:59,500 --> 00:29:03,500
I'd say if you look at the companies that we see,

474
00:29:03,500 --> 00:29:08,500
they're either doing a lot of revenue and growing revenue really fast on the traditional side

475
00:29:08,500 --> 00:29:12,500
if they're carrying valuations of that sort or they're profitable.

476
00:29:12,500 --> 00:29:14,500
And I don't think you say as much on the protocol side.

477
00:29:14,500 --> 00:29:16,500
There's still a gap there.

478
00:29:16,500 --> 00:29:20,500
This is where it gets a little sticky because revenue begets revenue.

479
00:29:20,500 --> 00:29:26,500
So you're never going to make money if you invest in a trend once it's already obvious.

480
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:29,500
Now, tons of crypto valuations are inflated,

481
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:34,500
but I think a lot of the valuations are really a function of the expectation of future revenues

482
00:29:34,500 --> 00:29:37,500
rather than the presence of current revenues.

483
00:29:37,500 --> 00:29:42,500
So it's the idea that does this company have a probability of succeeding in capturing a market?

484
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:44,500
And if it captures that market, what is that market worth?

485
00:29:44,500 --> 00:29:47,500
And if that market's in five years or eight years,

486
00:29:47,500 --> 00:29:50,500
sure, you can do your DCF and try to figure out what it's going to be worth,

487
00:29:50,500 --> 00:29:53,500
but you don't know what the size of anything is in five or eight years.

488
00:29:53,500 --> 00:29:58,500
So your DCF is going to be just as good as the valuation that the market is willing to buy and sell it at.

489
00:29:58,500 --> 00:30:02,500
And then to complicate this, you have simple supply and demand issues.

490
00:30:02,500 --> 00:30:08,500
So the supply of flow that is out there for quality projects is so low,

491
00:30:08,500 --> 00:30:16,500
and the demand for these safe assets is so high that you have premiums and you have to pay a premium.

492
00:30:16,500 --> 00:30:19,500
And it's not like VCs are immune to paying premiums.

493
00:30:19,500 --> 00:30:22,500
When you see a really hot team go and raise capital,

494
00:30:22,500 --> 00:30:25,500
the VCs get priced out by other VCs as well.

495
00:30:26,500 --> 00:30:29,500
In a market like this, it's really bizarre.

496
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:37,500
We see one really good team that's not so high, raising at $12.5 million, that has traction, has done a lot of the work.

497
00:30:37,500 --> 00:30:43,500
I see another team, it's a good team, just out of the gates, hasn't done anything yet, raising at $30 million.

498
00:30:43,500 --> 00:30:49,500
And then I see another, it's a great team, literally hasn't done anything yet, raising at $50 million.

499
00:30:49,500 --> 00:30:54,500
So what you see in how these VCs are funding these things is they're paying a huge premium for the talent.

500
00:30:54,500 --> 00:30:56,500
Because the talent is the scarce thing.

501
00:30:56,500 --> 00:30:59,500
There's a prescribed probabilism to it.

502
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:03,500
Exactly. And maybe we put our probabilities a little bit too high.

503
00:31:03,500 --> 00:31:04,500
That's the case.

504
00:31:04,500 --> 00:31:06,500
Crypto is an optimistic industry.

505
00:31:06,500 --> 00:31:09,500
Recently, it hasn't felt that way, but generally speaking, it is.

506
00:31:09,500 --> 00:31:11,500
A number goes up in our minds.

507
00:31:11,500 --> 00:31:14,500
So yeah, we're going to wait our probabilities a little bit high.

508
00:31:14,500 --> 00:31:15,500
These things are going to be overpriced.

509
00:31:15,500 --> 00:31:21,500
If you look at the lending markets, I would love to sit here and tell you that I think the lending markets are underpriced categorically.

510
00:31:22,500 --> 00:31:24,500
I think that they're underpriced, but not categorically.

511
00:31:24,500 --> 00:31:28,500
And I think you have to have a five-year investment horizon for that to be the case.

512
00:31:28,500 --> 00:31:33,500
But if you put in a two or three-year investment horizon into my model, it will tell you that you're going to be overpaying.

513
00:31:33,500 --> 00:31:37,500
So the question is, do you want to be overpaying now or you want to be overpaying in the future?

514
00:31:37,500 --> 00:31:47,500
Yeah, I think the question we ask sometimes on this, which I don't have a great answer for, to be fair, is if you combine the market caps of all those $200 million protocols,

515
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:53,500
the fictional ones that we were talking about, of the layer ones, of the leading lending protocols,

516
00:31:53,500 --> 00:32:00,500
and you compile them into the winning cohort in five years, which numbers more?

517
00:32:00,500 --> 00:32:06,500
Because you would argue if AVE becomes big enough, it's a trillion dollar token or a hundred billion dollar token,

518
00:32:06,500 --> 00:32:13,500
as long as that exceeds the total value that everyone's paying for these things today, then you can justify that probabilism.

519
00:32:13,500 --> 00:32:20,500
I think wrapped into that is hand waving over a lot of these ghost coins that we all know is not worth the money it is.

520
00:32:20,500 --> 00:32:26,500
And probably the reason that these things are at this value, it's not like this is changing hands often.

521
00:32:26,500 --> 00:32:34,500
The reason that they're at this value is because the holders either lost their coins or they can't do anything with the money anyways.

522
00:32:34,500 --> 00:32:37,500
Maybe it's dark money. So they just sit in these protocols.

523
00:32:37,500 --> 00:32:42,500
Are you talking about the DeFi protocols or are you talking about Bitcoin Cash and Hedera Hashgraph?

524
00:32:42,500 --> 00:32:48,500
Yeah, the NEOs, the Dino coins. If you look at any of the teams that are shipping in DeFi

525
00:32:48,500 --> 00:32:53,500
and you look at the collective valuation of these, I think you would look at this valuation and be like,

526
00:32:53,500 --> 00:32:57,500
this is not even close to what the TAM is if this thing succeeds.

527
00:32:57,500 --> 00:33:04,500
And then the question just becomes, how do you go from a early stage protocol to monetization?

528
00:33:04,500 --> 00:33:06,500
And this is where we see a lot of the teams struggle.

529
00:33:06,500 --> 00:33:15,500
And this is why I pass on a lot of deals, is because the leaps that you have to take to make this either more than what it is today

530
00:33:15,500 --> 00:33:21,500
or to be able to actually charge your users more without getting disintermediated, it's a stretch probabilistically.

531
00:33:21,500 --> 00:33:23,500
So the valuation is too high.

532
00:33:23,500 --> 00:33:26,500
Did you see a CEEPS tweet the other day on Monad?

533
00:33:26,500 --> 00:33:29,500
Yeah, the probability thinking for valuation.

534
00:33:29,500 --> 00:33:33,500
And just the justification that when you're paying these sorts of premiums,

535
00:33:33,500 --> 00:33:41,500
you are justifying it in the scope of the winning outcome of how large a thing this can be.

536
00:33:41,500 --> 00:33:47,500
And he believes that a lot of the pessimistic thinking we're getting today is a loss of that belief in the upside.

537
00:33:47,500 --> 00:33:51,500
I think he's incredibly right. The other day somebody asked me, they looked at me and they said,

538
00:33:51,500 --> 00:33:55,500
do you think Ethereum is really worth $300 billion?

539
00:33:55,500 --> 00:34:02,500
And this is a tough question to answer because Ethereum, there's nobody above it when you look at smart contracts.

540
00:34:02,500 --> 00:34:07,500
So this is the ceiling. This is when Asib talks about what's the probability Monad becomes the next Ethereum,

541
00:34:07,500 --> 00:34:09,500
should the valuation be some percentage of that?

542
00:34:09,500 --> 00:34:12,500
What he's not asking is, what is Ethereum worth?

543
00:34:12,500 --> 00:34:17,500
He's just saying the market values at this, consider the market to be completely sane.

544
00:34:17,500 --> 00:34:21,500
I think he's saying there's this much money in the market, which maybe we're saying the same thing,

545
00:34:21,500 --> 00:34:28,500
but I'm to some extent a believer that a lot of these valuations applied are based on the supply of capital.

546
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:32,500
And the supply of capital is saying that whether it is Ethereum or something else,

547
00:34:32,500 --> 00:34:38,500
that's the amount of capital that's going to go into the premium L1 or L1s.

548
00:34:38,500 --> 00:34:44,500
Maybe I would ask you to share your thoughts on stablecoins and how stablecoins impact the valuation.

549
00:34:44,500 --> 00:34:46,500
Of other altcoins?

550
00:34:46,500 --> 00:34:47,500
Yeah.

551
00:34:47,500 --> 00:34:52,500
I think stablecoins, tokenized assets, and meme coins, frankly,

552
00:34:52,500 --> 00:34:58,500
a broader range of tokens are materially detrimental to altcoins from this liquidity perspective.

553
00:34:58,500 --> 00:35:00,500
I think that's de facto true.

554
00:35:00,500 --> 00:35:04,500
And this goes back to what I would argue is somewhat basic rates theory

555
00:35:04,500 --> 00:35:08,500
around the idea that equity markets struggle during higher rates environments

556
00:35:08,500 --> 00:35:11,500
and during lower rate environments you see more speculation.

557
00:35:11,500 --> 00:35:15,500
I think that's purely because of that liquidity struggle. I think it has to be.

558
00:35:15,500 --> 00:35:18,500
It's very easy to get pessimistic in crypto.

559
00:35:18,500 --> 00:35:23,500
And in an environment like this, it's a weird bear market because it feels like the valuations are quite high,

560
00:35:23,500 --> 00:35:28,500
but it also feels like across the board sentiment literally cannot be worse.

561
00:35:28,500 --> 00:35:29,500
Within crypto?

562
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:31,500
Yeah. Outside of crypto, people are optimistic.

563
00:35:31,500 --> 00:35:38,500
Exactly. And the thing that I always like to remind myself is crypto sits so far out on the risk curve

564
00:35:38,500 --> 00:35:42,500
that it is one macro trade. It is essentially one macro trade.

565
00:35:42,500 --> 00:35:46,500
This is why we don't like crypto hedge funds who say that they're macro funds doing crypto.

566
00:35:46,500 --> 00:35:51,500
It's because just by being in crypto, you have taken a macro outlook and you made that bet.

567
00:35:51,500 --> 00:35:56,500
If you were a real macro fund, you would be in TradFi and then you would maybe bet on crypto sometimes.

568
00:35:56,500 --> 00:35:58,500
You wouldn't just be in crypto.

569
00:35:58,500 --> 00:36:05,500
So what happens to us is obviously we get impacted by interest rates probably the most out of any sector.

570
00:36:05,500 --> 00:36:07,500
Because we're a risk asset?

571
00:36:07,500 --> 00:36:14,500
We're not just a risk asset. We're a risk asset that's extremely sensitive to rates.

572
00:36:14,500 --> 00:36:18,500
So you have to recognize where we are in the world of rates.

573
00:36:18,500 --> 00:36:24,500
It's no surprise that the last real bull market, COVID, was zero interest rates.

574
00:36:24,500 --> 00:36:29,500
And ever since then, the US has just been tightening and tightening and tightening its belt.

575
00:36:29,500 --> 00:36:33,500
And within the past couple months, we've started to see that change.

576
00:36:33,500 --> 00:36:36,500
I don't think there's a better bull case for crypto, period.

577
00:36:36,500 --> 00:36:42,500
I don't disagree, but the irony of all this is crypto springs from the disillusionment of people,

578
00:36:42,500 --> 00:36:49,500
maybe even call people the libertarian lean of monetary policy carried out by governments, the US government,

579
00:36:49,500 --> 00:36:55,500
and thus thrives when monetary policy is arguably problematic, reckless.

580
00:36:55,500 --> 00:37:02,500
So when the crypto people get arguably what they would want, which is a more responsible monetary policy,

581
00:37:02,500 --> 00:37:08,500
crypto underperforms because of the fact that then you actually have a more reasonable monetary policy

582
00:37:08,500 --> 00:37:11,500
that lessens the supposed value of crypto.

583
00:37:11,500 --> 00:37:14,500
Crypto is essentially the short trader in the crew.

584
00:37:14,500 --> 00:37:18,500
Shout out to Fozzie. He's only happy when everybody else is sad.

585
00:37:18,500 --> 00:37:24,500
Because essentially crypto does great when we're in hyperinflationary environment and rates are zero.

586
00:37:24,500 --> 00:37:29,500
Exactly. So that's what I mean by the whole industry is a macro trade.

587
00:37:29,500 --> 00:37:31,500
There's no way to be a macro fund in crypto.

588
00:37:31,500 --> 00:37:36,500
You only have one mechanism, which is go long and then you could go short theoretically,

589
00:37:36,500 --> 00:37:38,500
but it's just one implementation of one viewpoint.

590
00:37:38,500 --> 00:37:40,500
So maybe we can tie this back.

591
00:37:40,500 --> 00:37:44,500
I think this is also another interesting bull case for lending.

592
00:37:44,500 --> 00:37:48,500
And it's a little counterintuitive, but you take it one step and it makes sense.

593
00:37:48,500 --> 00:37:56,500
The lending markets on the surface as interest rates lower in the US, the lending rates should go down.

594
00:37:56,500 --> 00:38:01,500
The center is Paribas. That means that lending markets make less money.

595
00:38:01,500 --> 00:38:06,500
But what you see happen when that occurs is interest rates go down,

596
00:38:06,500 --> 00:38:11,500
the borrow cost on lending markets will go down, and the volume of borrow should go up

597
00:38:11,500 --> 00:38:16,500
because all of a sudden strategies that we see that were not possible at certain rates

598
00:38:16,500 --> 00:38:19,500
become much more durable, much more possible.

599
00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:21,500
So you start to see a volume uptick.

600
00:38:21,500 --> 00:38:26,500
That volume uptick can be exacerbated by the fact that you have this summer,

601
00:38:26,500 --> 00:38:30,500
the seasonality coming back and you start to see swap fees go up.

602
00:38:30,500 --> 00:38:34,500
You start to see other indicators that this market is recovering.

603
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:37,500
And when that happens, your basis trade comes back.

604
00:38:37,500 --> 00:38:40,500
So all of a sudden your basis trade, the yield is going up

605
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:45,500
because you have a lot of people in crypto who are trading purpose and exaggerating price movements.

606
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:47,500
And your lending rates are going down,

607
00:38:47,500 --> 00:38:53,500
which means that the yield you can make in this really vanilla strategy with a couple loops

608
00:38:53,500 --> 00:38:56,500
actually looks really attractive on a global level,

609
00:38:56,500 --> 00:38:59,500
especially when you compare it to T-bills which have fallen off a cliff.

610
00:38:59,500 --> 00:39:02,500
So I really think lending markets are interesting

611
00:39:02,500 --> 00:39:07,500
because in the high interest rate environment, they benefit from high interest rates.

612
00:39:07,500 --> 00:39:11,500
In the low interest rate environment, they benefit from low interest rates.

613
00:39:11,500 --> 00:39:14,500
To me, it has all of the workings of a really strong business.

614
00:39:14,500 --> 00:39:17,500
We could talk a little bit about some of the pitfalls I think we will see

615
00:39:17,500 --> 00:39:19,500
and where the lending market is going.

616
00:39:19,500 --> 00:39:21,500
I'm curious if you have thoughts on that.

617
00:39:21,500 --> 00:39:22,500
I'm aligned.

